  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Anomalously warm temperatures tonight and \nFriday could approach daily record highs.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA warm front associated with the northern low pressure system\nwill stretch across NB through tonight and Friday. The warm,\nmoist airmass will continue to move into the region with the SSW\nflow. For tonight, low temps are expected to linger in the 50s\nacross the region. Friday will feature another round of\nanomalously warm temperatures at least 10 degrees above average,\nhowever increasing cloud cover ahead of the next low pressure\nsystem and decreased warm air advection may lead to temperatures\nnot quite as warm as earlier in the week, limiting but not\ncompletely eliminating the chance for reaching daily record high\ntemperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Additional rainfall expected Friday into the \nweekend, though amounts are still uncertain. The rainfall could \nhelp provide some drought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA low pressure system from the Great Lakes region moves east on\nFriday. This system helps develop a coastal low that will move\nNE along the New England coast. As of this update, the ensembles\nseem to agree with the frontal boundary across Downeast on\nFriday. Then as the low merges and intensifies, rain amounts\nincrease across the south. By Saturday, the low moves NE,\nspreading rain across the region. The models are in better\nagreement with the center of the low tracking along the Gulf of\nMaine and into the Bay of Fundy through the weekend. This will\nbring continued rain throughout the area. Total rain amounts are\nstill uncertain, but starting to look like 1 to 2 inches for the\nsouth and 0.25 to 0.75 inches for the north.",
